  "reading_note": "",
  "illustration_prompt": "Create a single publication-quality landscape 3:2 narrative illustration for an adult illustrated English-reading booklet. Finished standalone artwork, not a book mockup, not a collage of multiple unrelated panels. Sophisticated contemporary editorial gouache and watercolor, carefully controlled colored-pencil accents, velvety matte paint, subtle warm paper texture, soft natural daylight. Warm ivory paper dominates; subdued eucalyptus and sage greens structure the composition; restrained buttery lemon-yellow and dusty terracotta accents. Calm, comfortable, refined, credible drawing and natural anatomy. An airy coherent composition with one clear story focal point and at most two supporting details, generous breathing room at all edges and softly feathered painted edges into ivory. Detailed but uncluttered. Historically and scientifically appropriate objects, clothing and setting. Show the particular article's pivotal event or mechanism rather than a generic portrait or generic textbook symbols. Human figures treated with dignity; no grotesque caricature, no sensational violence or visible injuries. For disasters, emphasize the setting, evidence or human response without graphic suffering. For uncertain scientific claims, depict the observations and investigation without presenting debated mechanisms as proven. Absolutely no typography, captions, letters, numbers, logos, watermarks, diagram labels, fake writing or invented charts anywhere. Do not render invisible forces as literal magic. Avoid corporate vector icons, slick 3D rendering and childish cartoon style. High-quality, print-ready visual storytelling, matching a warm ivory-and-sage adult watercolor booklet.\n\nARTICLE67: The actual discovery of prehistoric Lascaux animal paintings by four French teenage boys in September 1940, not a dog rescue. One coherent quiet limestone cave interior, focal point a softly illuminated ochre-and-charcoal wall bearing a few credible prehistoric side-profile bulls and horses, clearly animal images, not human portraits. Four distinct teenage explorers in plain period French trousers and jackets, seen mostly in three-quarter backs or silhouettes, stand a respectful distance from the wall and look with quiet wonder. One carries a modest period hand lamp; no modern LED headgear, safety helmets, ropes or smartphones. Do not show anyone touching or redrawing the fragile paintings. Omit the dog from the interior entirely: it sniffed at the entrance and did not fall into the cave. No injury, theatrical treasure, text, graffiti or invented symbols. The wall and the observers are the story, subdued warm ivory limestone, sage-grey clothing and restrained terracotta pigments, adult watercolor with natural anatomy and generous negative space.",
